      "writtenOn": "2022-08-18T05:43:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Done.\n\nA part of me wonders if we should disallow `writeonly` on storage blocks. GLSL and SPIR-V (via the NonReadable decoration) have a nice equivalent but I don\u0027t know of a good way to enforce this for WGSL and MSL (WGSL only allows `read` and `read_write` on storage buffer variables, see https://www.w3.org/TR/WGSL/#var-decls and Metal allows you to declare a buffer parameter with or without `const`).\n\n`readonly` is universally supported by the target languages but `writeonly` isn\u0027t. We can still enforce this via frontend checks on variable references (IIRC we don\u0027t do this for either keyword) so maybe it doesn\u0027t matter that only some backends will generate truly writeonly buffers.",
      "parentUuid": "4bd47472_9245a56a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 24,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "51576e9e1205e2128b37999cff4e4695c4703f5e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "41e22d53_3c937e0a",
        "filename": "resources/sksl/compute/ArrayAdd.compute",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-18T14:11:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`readonly` and `writeonly` are brand new, so we don\u0027t do any real enforcement of them in the front-end yet. There\u0027s skia:13627 to track this work for textures; you should probably file a similar tracking bug if there\u0027s similar work to do for storage buffers.\n\nI think as long as the frontend is enforcing things properly, it\u0027s fine if a particular backend does nothing for `readonly` and `writeonly`.",
      "parentUuid": "ef4064b4_b7b4d884",
