    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "afa70453_708c5399",
        "filename": "tests/sksl/compute/ArrayAdd.metal",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 5631
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-16T13:40:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There\u0027s a comment about it earlier in the CL. I think we only have two good options:\n  - This one, where we convert the unsized arrays to being size 1.\n  - Omitting the array member entirely, and then adding helpers that compute offsets from the buffer address.\n  \n  I think the current ([1]) solution is probably involves much less rewriting of variable references, etc... ?",
      "parentUuid": "30faa5d7_d2630ef6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 15
      },
      "revId": "51576e9e1205e2128b37999cff4e4695c4703f5e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f104f3c8_6a12901b",
        "filename": "tests/sksl/compute/ArrayAdd.metal",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-18T05:43:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I essentially made Brian\u0027s suggestion in the comments on line 2295 in src/sksl/codegen/SkSLMetalCodeGenerator.cpp in this CL. To summarize, variable length array at the end of a struct (or \"flexible arrays\") is a C99 feature and technically not permitted in C++ but most compilers allow it. The common C++ approach to this idiom is to declare a one element array as the last member of the struct and compilers generally generate code that \"works as expected\".\n\nThis works as expected (at least on M1 Pro but I\u0027m pretty sure this works in other architectures too) but I think a good follow-up would be to omit the array member and instead explicitly compute the offset for each variable reference, respecting any implicit layout rules. This is because referencing past a struct in C++ leads to undefined behavior, though in Metal all struct-typed buffer pointers effectively work like a reinterpret_cast so we\u0027re always in UB territory. FWIW the output above matches tint.\n\n---\nThere is another alternative which is to rely on an argument buffer. This has implications on how graphite populates the buffers (it would need to use a special Metal API function and makes sure that the unsized array portion of the SSBO lives in a separate dedicated buffer) but the declaration above would effectively become while keeping the entry point declaration the same:\n\n    struct inputBlock {\n        uint offset;\n        const device int* src;\n    };",
      "parentUuid": "afa70453_708c5399",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 15
      },
      "revId": "51576e9e1205e2128b37999cff4e4695c4703f5e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "94128bd0_66b568f6",
        "filename": "tests/sksl/compute/ArrayAdd.metal",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-18T15:46:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We should ask Apple if they have any recommendations here. Switching to pointers adds an extra indirection, so it would be better to keep the arrays IMO, but obviously we want to generate conceptually-correct code too!\n\nFor the time being I\u0027m fine with `[1]` especially if we\u0027re not the first ones to do it.",
      "parentUuid": "f104f3c8_6a12901b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
